CVE-2026-75604: Fix incremental-cache path traversal (backslash segments)

Next.js apps using Pages Router or App Router on Windows-hosted servers were
exposed to path traversal (CWE-22) leading to disclosure of the
server-reference-manifest encryption key and potential RCE. Backslash path
separators in attacker-controlled route segments were not sanitized and the
incremental-cache file path was allowed to escape the intended app root.

This backport of the upstream fix (vercel/next.js b0f3460a9 / 968b9fcb2) is
applied to the compiled artifacts shipped in the published next@14.2.10 npm
package (dist/ CJS and dist/esm/ ESM builds), which is what the native npm
patcher extracts and rebuilds:
  * escapePathDelimiters now escapes backslashes ("\\" and encoded "%5c") in
    addition to "/", "#" and "?" so Windows path separators cannot survive as
    literal separators in cache keys.
  * FileSystemCache.getFilePath now computes a per-kind rootDir and rejects any
    resolved path that escapes that root (startsWith(rootDir + path.sep) guard),
    throwing "Invalid file path" instead of reading/writing outside the cache
    root.

